# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Validated by lib/env.ts at boot.
# Anything missing or malformed fails fast rather than at runtime.

# --- Supabase ---------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server only. Never expose to the browser, never log, never put in an AI prompt.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- App --------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Do NOT set NODE_ENV here. Next sets it itself: development for `next dev`,
# production for `next build`. Because Next loads .env.local during a build too,
# a NODE_ENV pinned in this file overrides the production value and the build
# resolves React inconsistently, which fails prerendering /_global-error with
# "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'useContext')". That error names a
# Next internal page and looks like a framework bug, so it costs an afternoon to
# trace back to this line. A unit test now asserts this file does not set it.

# --- Observability (optional until step A5) ---------------------------------
SENTRY_DSN=

# --- Credential encryption (required from step A5) --------------------------
# Seals marketplace credentials at rest. Required as soon as a real channel is
# connected; optional before that, so CI and a fresh checkout run without one.
#
# Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# This holds a KEYRING, not a single key. A bare base64 key means version 1,
# which is what a deployment that has never rotated looks like. To rotate, put
# the new key first and keep the old entry until no row still uses it:
#
#   CREDENTIALS_ENCRYPTION_KEY="2:<new base64>,1:<old base64>"
#
# The full procedure is docs/decisions/0003-credential-key-rotation.md. Retiring
# an entry that rows still reference is the one irreversible step.
CREDENTIALS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Shopify (step A5) ------------------------------------------------------
# From the Shopify Dev Dashboard. Legacy custom apps could not be created after
# 1 January 2026, so new apps go through the dashboard; a merchant can install an
# unlisted app without App Store review, which is the alpha path.
#
# The app's redirect URI must be registered as exactly:
#   <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL>/api/channels/shopify/oauth/callback
#
# And its access scopes must be configured as exactly:
#   write_products,read_products,read_publications,write_publications
#
# The app configuration is the CEILING, not the request. Scopes asked for in
# the authorization URL that the app does not declare are dropped silently:
# the grant succeeds, the token comes back short, and the first call needing
# the missing scope fails. Adding a scope means releasing a new version of the
# app in the Dev Dashboard and then reconnecting the store. Verified the hard
# way on 7 September 2026 — a reconnect that requested four scopes returned
# one.
#
# Both are optional: without them the Shopify card says the channel is not
# configured on this deployment, and nothing else changes. They are parsed in the
# adapter rather than in lib/env.ts, so an app with no Shopify credentials is
# still a valid app.
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SHOPIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- AI merchandising (step B1) ---------------------------------------------
# The model vendor's key. Optional: without it the listing page says composing
# is not configured on this deployment and nothing else changes. Parsed in
# lib/ai/providers, not in lib/env.ts, for the same reason the channel
# credentials are: the vendor's name stays inside the provider folder.
#
# Server only. Never exposed to the browser, never logged, and never placed in
# a prompt — the prompt carries the FactSheet and the channel profile and
# nothing from the environment.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Background jobs (step B1) ----------------------------------------------
# Trigger.dev. Optional: without a secret key lib/jobs keeps the in-process
# queue, which is what CI, the database test suite and a fresh checkout use.
# With it, every job is a Trigger.dev task (trigger/jobs.ts) and survives the
# request that started it. Run `pnpm jobs:dev` beside `pnpm dev` to execute
# tasks locally against the dev environment.
#
# The project ref is read by trigger.config.ts. It is not a secret.
TRIGGER_SECRET_KEY=
TRIGGER_PROJECT_REF=

# --- Etsy (step A6) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Approved 8 September 2026: the developer app and commercial access. From the
# app's page at https://www.etsy.com/developers/your-apps.
#
# Etsy calls these the KEYSTRING and the SHARED SECRET. The keystring is the
# OAuth client_id; the shared secret is never sent in the OAuth flow (Etsy uses
# PKCE) but every v3 API call carries both in one header, `x-api-key:
# <keystring>:<shared secret>`, so both are needed.
#
# The app's callback URL must be registered as exactly, case-sensitive:
#   <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL>/api/channels/etsy/oauth/callback
#
# The scopes the adapter asks for are listings_r, listings_w, listings_d and
# shops_r; listings_d is for the compensating delete a failed publish performs
# on its own draft. transactions_r arrives at B5 and, as with every scope added later,
# means every connected shop re-authorizes. Access tokens last an hour and the
# refresh token ninety days, so the adapter refreshes; a shop untouched for
# ninety days has to be reconnected.
#
# Both are optional: without them the Etsy card says the channel is not
# configured on this deployment. Parsed in the adapter, not in lib/env.ts.
ETSY_CLIENT_ID=
ETSY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Billing (step C1) ------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe. Optional as a whole: without a secret key the settings page says
# billing is not configured on this deployment, every workspace runs without
# a subscription, and the ledger rows a connection writes stay pending. Once
# the key is set, all six are required, and a missing one fails at first use
# naming the variable.
#
# The four prices are created in the dashboard to match lib/billing/rules.ts:
# a $9 monthly and $90 yearly base at quantity one, and a $6 monthly and $60
# yearly per-unit channel price. The adapter tells the two items on a
# subscription apart by price id, so these have to be the ids of those prices
# and not of their products.
#
# The webhook endpoint is:
#   <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL>/api/billing/webhook
# subscribed to customer.subscription.created, customer.subscription.updated,
# customer.subscription.deleted and checkout.session.completed. The signing
# secret from that endpoint is STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET. For local development,
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/billing/webhook` prints one.
#
# The customer portal has to be enabled in the dashboard, with cancellation
# and the monthly/yearly switch allowed, because Fanwise builds none of those
# screens.
#
# Server only. Parsed in lib/billing/providers, not in lib/env.ts, so the
# vendor's name stays inside the provider folder.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_PRICE_BASE_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_BASE_YEARLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_CHANNEL_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_CHANNEL_YEARLY=
